  "query_preview": "..\n\nLast vestiges of upper shortwave ridging are departing this morning, \nwith remnant warm mid levels maintaining stable conditions through \nnoon. A closed upper low over the arrowhead of MN is on track to \npass across Upper MI later today, opening into a trough in the \nprocess. Weak moisture transport ensues on modest S to SW wind, \nculminating in a more humid day with dew points creeping into the \nmid to upper 60s by this afternoon. As the upper trough erodes the \nremnant stability, showers and storms should percolate along a \nsurface trough into west MI late this morning. Relatively weak \ndivergence aloft and gradual height falls provide support for ascent \nas convection spreads eastward along the lead edge of the inbound \nlow-level theta-e ridge, bringing fairly high confidence (60-70%) \nfor showers and thunderstorms this afternoon and evening. Timing in \nhi-res guidance continues to favor arrival into the Tri-Cities \naround noon to 3pm, then the rest of SE MI between 3pm and 10pm.\n\nHREF shows mean SBCAPE of 1000-1500 J/kg building in but with \nconvection generally outpacing the higher instability as the evening \ngoes on. Bulk wind shear on the order of 25-30 kt will offer \npotential for marginal updraft organization and a window for \nisolated stronger to severe storms. A band of slightly higher SW \n850mb flow will accompany the trough, enlarging low level hodographs \nto some degree especially if surface wind remains backed/southerly. \nDamaging wind gusts will be the primary threat in the strongest \nstorms, but large hail and a weak tornado will also be possible if \nany supercellular structures emerge. Hi-res ML guidance continues to \nfavor areas south and west of SE MI for this threat, and will note \nthat neighborhood probabilities for all severe hazards have \ndecreased in 00z HREF. The SWODY1 slight risk area south of I-96 is \ndriven by 15% wind probs, with hi-res reflectivity suggestive of \ntransient linear features in this area. Localized heavy downpours \nwill be possible as PWAT flirts with the 90th percentile around \n1.50\", but ensemble QPF data do not indicate a broader excessive \nrainfall threat.\n\nA lull in convection is anticipated by late evening as the initial \nsurface trough works eastward, but lingering instability ahead of \nthe cold front will offer potential for additional spottier showers \nand perhaps a thunderstorm into the early overnight. The cold front \nthen passes through early morning to end precip chances. The inbound \nair mass is not much different in temperature but slightly less \nhumid. Weak instability develops on Thursday as longwave troughing \ninduces steeper lapse rates aloft - this fuels ~20% probability for \nisolated late afternoon and evening showers.\n\nA secondary cold front drops through ahead of high pressure that \nbuilds in through Friday and Saturday. This brings pleasant and less \nhumid conditions with lots of sunshine. By early next week, the \nsubtropical ridge over the southern Plains is progged to migrate \ninto the Tennessee Valley. Warmer southwest flow riding in north of \nthe ridge will bring a warming trend next week with the latest NBM \nadvertising a 40-50% chance for parts of SE MI to see highs in \nexcess of 90 degrees Monday and Tuesday.",
